WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.800
Neurons communicate with each other.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.300
relaying messages throughout your body and powering all of your thoughts and actions.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.100
Neurons talk to each other using both electrical and chemical signals.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.600
Messages start as electrical signals traveling rapidly down a neuron.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.300
These signals are called action potentials.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.800
When they reach the gap between two neurons, the messages need some help to get across.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.139
The information is transformed from an action potential into a chemical message,

00:00:29.139 --> 00:00:31.400
which crosses the gap called a synapse.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:38.179
The release of those chemical messengers can trigger an action potential in the neuron on the other side of the synapse,

00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:39.600
conveying the message onward.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.900
or it can quiet the message.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.300
This happens over and over and over.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.019
And with repeated activity, the synapse gets stronger,

00:00:51.019 --> 00:00:54.000
so the next message is more likely to get through.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.100
neurons learn to pass on important messages and ignore the rest.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.600
This is how our brains learn and adapt to an ever-changing world.
